  "name": "LIPOPROTEIN ELECTROPHORESIS",
  "code": "1145",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul class=\"marker:text-textOff list-disc\">\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><strong>Normal Pattern:</strong>&nbsp;Clear serum with predominant Beta-lipoprotein band; faint Pre-Beta and Alpha bands; negligible or absent chylomicrons.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><strong>Type II Hyperlipoproteinemia:</strong>&nbsp;Elevated Beta (LDL) band; associated with hypercholesterolemia.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><strong>Type III (Broad Beta) Pattern:</strong>&nbsp;Broad Beta band with presence of abnormal lipoprotein (&ldquo;floating Beta&rdquo;); elevated cholesterol and triglycerides; plasma may be turbid.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><strong>Type IV Pattern:</strong>&nbsp;Increased Pre-Beta (VLDL); elevated triglycerides; Alpha and Beta bands usually normal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><strong>Chylomicrons Present:</strong>&nbsp;Suggests postprandial lipemia or Type I hyperlipoproteinemia.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p class=\"my-0\"><strong>Additional Comments:</strong> Abnormal lipoprotein patterns may be primary (genetic) or secondary to metabolic disorders (e.g., diabetes, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
